A||N||9||tgowens||Grand Am Radio in a Fiero -- HELP!!||||1||
Z||000000||tgowens||06-29-2000||10:07 PM||||ok, I put in an AM/FM/CD w/ equalizer in my '87GT right? well, it sure does sound good but.......anybody out there with a Grand Am (this came out of a '98 Grand Am) or any other Pontiac who can tell me how to preset a radio station on this thing?? There is no set button but this is the one with the HR/MIN buttons on the lower right of the front of the unit........any help would be appreciated as I don't have the manual for this radio......Thanks!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bill Osmer||06-29-2000||10:26 PM||||Press on either the "H" or the "M" button for 2 - 5 seconds. The respective digits will begin flashing - then the display is in set mode. Continue pressing either the "H" or "M" to adjust time. When the time is set, just sit back - in approx 10 seconds, the digits will stop flashing and the clock will have the desired time entered.||||reg||10
Z||000002||terryk||06-29-2000||10:59 PM||||To set stations, just press and hold the number button of interested until it goes back to the station frequency. About 5 seconds or so. Very simple.||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||06-29-2000||11:08 PM||||Bill - thanks for the info, I knew how to set the time tho, it was the stations I didn't know how to set.<P>terryk - thanks a bunch!! imagine this, I built my own computer but I couldn't set the stations on my radio!!! thanks man, I appreciate it!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||BN Boomer||06-30-2000||08:07 AM||||Hey, cough it up, Terry. How was the install of the Grand Am radio? Did you need an adapter for the wiring harness? Hopefully the radio I'm buying from a guy who pulled it out of his 97 Sunfire will arrive today.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-30-2000||09:36 AM||||Don't feel bad.  I still have trouble setting the clock on my VCR.  (I know.  "I remember VCRs from the <I>old</I> days.")<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000006||tgowens||06-30-2000||12:19 PM||||Boomer, the install was a simple one and yes, you need the adapter (for the wiring harness as well as for the antenna lead) -- it is GM part #16140051 and lists for $18.25 from a dealer.........I am having a slight problem getting the sub-woofer to work right tho (it is the stock unit with the stock amp) but i'll get that one figured out.<P>MM - ya mean there is a VCR in this dang radio?? man, where is it? How do I get the VCR tape in it -- I got the TV hooked up ok and I really don't care about the clock but where do I insert the tape??????||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||06-30-2000||01:02 PM||||There are three ways to approach the installation.<P>1. Get the adapter.<P>2. Find an old cassette player and remove the speaker connector/plugs and make the adapter.<P>3. Cut the wires and rewire. <P>YOu will need the larger to small antenna adapter available from any car audio store.<P>If you have the factory subwoofer, the power for it comes from the power antenna output of the radio (so it's turned on with the radio). I found that the Pontiac CD antenna output couldn't power the subwoofer, so I moved the sub's power to the switched ignition line to the CD player. The sub is always on with key on, but it's doesn't make any noises. <P>Other than needing some spacers to fit flush and the need to trim the ears for the mounting screws, that's it. It works great.<P>Terry<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 06-30-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000008||BN Boomer||06-30-2000||03:22 PM||||Thanks for the update, Terry. Being as I like clean installs and just doing things the right way in general, I'll order the adapter, fork over the $20, and wait a week for it to get here. I'll be sure to post some pics when I'm done so everyone can see what we're talking about. I'm also investigating a lead on another one of these radios. Never know if I might have 2 Fieros some day!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||06-30-2000||04:23 PM||||Boomer, if you have to order the adapter you may want to order it from Bobby at Newell Electronics..... <A HREF="http://www.newellelectronics.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.newellelectronics.com/</A>  he only charges $15 plus the shipping and I have talked to him several times about these radios (unlocking them is his specialty!!) -- tell him I said "Hi".......also, if you are using the stock subwoofer, splice tyhe two pink wires from the sub amp into the yellow from the wiring harness (white from the new adapter) that will power the sub in the accessory and run position........that's the only mod I had to make (thanks terryk) -- outside of that you will probably find that the new radio + the sub amp tend to overpower the speaker so you will have to tone it down or (this is what i am going to do) replace the sub speaker......<P>let me know how yours turns out......||||reg||1
